# Cloudflare Pages reads this file and does not serve it.
#
# The bundle and the stylesheet have fixed names - the build always writes
# public/assets/app.js - so a browser holding a cached copy keeps running the
# old app after a deploy. Pages' default for static assets is
#
#   Cache-Control: public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate
#
# which meant a push could take up to four hours to reach anyone who had
# visited before. must-revalidate does not help: it only applies once the copy
# is already stale, not during those four hours. Measured on the live site
# right after a deploy - the server had the new bundle in 30 seconds and the
# browser went on running the old one.
#
# `no-cache` keeps the copy but revalidates it before every use. The ETag does
# the real work: an unchanged file answers 304 with no body, so this costs one
# small conditional request per load rather than re-downloading 186KB.
#
# Fonts are deliberately left on the default. They are self-hosted, effectively
# never change, and four hours of caching is a benefit there rather than a
# hazard. Same for /trips/ photos - replacing one under its old filename is the
# only way to be bitten, and that is rare enough to not be worth the request.

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/css/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
